<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wulfred</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 832</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Wulfred of Canterbury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wulfred was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 805 to 832. A reformer, he required the clergy of his cathedral to live a common life and sought to bring monasteries under episcopal control. His long dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia">Coenwulf of Mercia</a> over the ownership of Kentish minsters led to his exclusion from office for several years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wulfred.jpg" alt="Wulfred" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: St. Albans District Council, Julian Watters, 2014-05-01 16:07:14 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early-Medieval_coin,_penny_of_Wulfred,_Archbishop_of_Canterbury_(FindID_614314).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known of Wulfred before 803, when he attended a synod on the staff of Archbishop Æthelhard. He probably came from a wealthy noble family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a> with lands at Hayes, Harrow and Twickenham, though earlier historians thought him Kentish. He was archdeacon of Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, was named archbishop-elect at a synod on 26 July 805 and was consecrated on 3 August 805 or later that year. He may have owed his elevation to Mercia's dominance over Kent at the time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Modelling his reform on that of Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrodegang-of-metz">Chrodegang of Metz</a>, Wulfred required the cathedral clergy to eat together, hand their property to the chapter and keep the canonical hours, and by 813 he claimed to have rebuilt the community. Whether the clergy became canons or remained monks is unclear. He granted lands to the chapter on condition that it kept the new standards.</p><p>His dispute with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia">Coenwulf of Mercia</a> turned on whether laymen could control monasteries, above all the Kentish houses of Reculver and Minster-in-Thanet. In 814 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to see <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a>. The king deprived him of authority for some years, six by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>'s own account of 825, though four is perhaps more likely, and Wulfred and his community answered with embassies to the pope and forged charters. Around 820 Coenwulf forced an unfavourable settlement on him. Relations improved under Ceolwulf, whom Wulfred consecrated king in September 822, and a council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clofesho">Clofesho</a> under King Beornwulf settled the matter in Wulfred's favour in 825.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wulfred died in 832, probably on 24 March, leaving most of his wealth to his kinsman Werhard, who was to pass the lands to Christ Church. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Egbert of Wessex</a> took Kent his relations with the new rulers were cold. The question of lordship over monasteries was finally settled at Kingston in 838. He was the first archbishop to put his portrait on his coins, which never named the Mercian king. The historian N. P. Brooks judged from the poor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of the charters of his time that the clergy of the cathedral were not very literate in Latin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wulfred” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=822" aria-label="Canterbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4574 387 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Wulfred"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.28,1.08&amp;year=822">What was here in 822 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
